## Phase 1 â€” System brief

MIRA is an AI-assisted tool that helps a person turn their identity, preferences, and visual references into a practical direction for a personal-brand photoshoot. It does not decide whether the person is suitable for a job, service, benefit, or other opportunity. Its purpose is creative planning.

The user completes a structured questionnaire. The MVP uses approximately 20 questions: a group of scaled questions that contribute to five creative or personality dimensions, followed by scenario-based questions about colour, setting, light, energy, styling, and camera comfort. The user may also provide a name or contact details and upload personal photographs or inspiration images. The information comes directly from the user rather than from automated web scraping. Questionnaire answers, contact details, and identifiable photographs are personal data. Images of other identifiable people are also personal data. Some free-text answers or references could incidentally reveal sensitive information, although MIRA is not intended to request it.

The workflow receives the form submission, records relevant data, applies scoring logic, and sends the resulting profile and references to configured text- and image-generation services. The MVP uses Tally for input, n8n for workflow orchestration, Google Sheets and Google Docs for storage and reporting, and a MIRA web application hosted on Railway. The exact model names and versions used for text and image generation must be added to the production system inventory.

MIRA outputs a written â€œCreative DNAâ€ interpretation and recommendations for mood, colours, styling, lighting, location, posing, and shoot preparation. It can also produce a moodboard or five-image â€œBrand Worldâ€ containing synthetic images. The questionnaire scores are intermediate creative signals; they are not eligibility or risk scores. The output is a recommendation and generated content, not a binding decision.

The person portrayed is most directly affected because the output describes their identity and may shape how they present themselves publicly. A photographer or creative team may also rely on the report when preparing the shoot. The user and photographer are expected to review, edit, reject, or replace every recommendation before it is used. MIRA does not automatically book a shoot, set a price, publish an image, or take another consequential action.

The MVP was designed and configured by the MIRA Project 3 team using third-party platforms and generative-AI services. In production, MIRA could be offered directly to individuals or used by photographers and studios with their clients.

