## Gap analysis and remediation plan

### Gap 1 â€” First-interaction AI disclosure

- **Obligation:** Articles 50(1) and 50(5): inform the person clearly, distinguishably, accessibly, and no later than the first interaction that they are interacting with an AI system, unless this is already obvious.
- **Current state:** MIRA is presented as an AI-assisted product, but the MVP does not enforce a standard disclosure before the questionnaire is submitted or when the result appears.
- **Required state:** The disclosure must not depend only on marketing copy or a terms-of-use link.
- **Remediation:** Add a short notice above the first questionnaire action and repeat a concise label on the results page. Include accessible text, not only an icon. Store the approved wording in the product requirements and test that it appears on mobile and desktop.
- **Escalation needed?** **Yes â€” legal review** of the final wording and whether the obviousness exception could apply; product implementation does not need a conformity body.

### Gap 2 â€” Machine-readable provenance of generated content

- **Obligation:** Article 50(2): providers of systems generating synthetic text or images must ensure output is marked in a machine-readable format and detectable as AI-generated or manipulated, subject to the Articleâ€™s technical-feasibility and limited exceptions.
- **Current state:** MIRA has no verified record of the provenance mechanism supplied by each model or whether later workflow steps strip metadata or other markers.
- **Required state:** The responsible provider must be able to show that an effective, interoperable, robust, and reliable marking method is implemented as far as technically feasible.
- **Remediation:** Record the exact model and API version; obtain the vendorâ€™s Article 50 technical documentation; verify output metadata or watermarking; preserve it through storage, resizing, document generation, and download; add automated and manual release tests; document any technical limitation. Do not claim compliance merely because the API vendor claims compliance.
- **Escalation needed?** **Yes â€” AI engineer, vendor, and AI regulatory counsel.** Confirm the downstream-provider allocation and the effect of any transition rule before launch.

### Gap 3 â€” Visible labelling of realistic generated images

- **Obligation:** Articles 50(4) and 50(5) require a deployer to disclose AI-generated or manipulated image, audio, or video that constitutes a deep fake. The disclosure must be clear, distinguishable, accessible, and timely.
- **Current state:** Five-image Brand World outputs can be shown without a consistent visible â€œAI-generatedâ€ label. Some images may resemble the user and appear to be real photographs.
- **Required state:** In-scope images must carry an appropriate human-visible disclosure, including when the content is creative or artistic.
- **Remediation:** Place â€œAI-generated concept image â€” not an actual photographâ€ beside each generated image and in downloads. Require professional customers to keep the label if they show or publish the image. Add a pre-publication check asking whether the output resembles a real person or event and could appear authentic.
- **Escalation needed?** **Yes â€” legal review** for edge cases; routine labelling can be implemented by product/design.

### Gap 4 â€” GDPR lawful-basis and transparency package

- **Obligation:** GDPR principles and Articles 6 and 13 require a defined purpose, lawful basis, data minimisation, and transparent information. Article 9 requires an additional condition if special-category data is intentionally processed.
- **Current state:** The MVP can collect identity answers, contact details, and identifiable photographs through multiple services, but no consolidated record explains each field, purpose, legal basis, recipient, retention period, or user right.
- **Required state:** Each data element and processing step must be necessary, justified, and explained before collection.
- **Remediation:** Create a data-flow inventory; remove non-essential fields; choose and document a lawful basis for each purpose; prohibit unnecessary sensitive-data submissions; draft an Article 13 privacy notice; create consent wording where consent is genuinely the correct basis; and define handling for third-party faces in uploaded images.
- **Escalation needed?** **Yes â€” data-protection counsel or DPO.** The DPO should also screen whether the combination of profiling and image processing requires a DPIA under Article 35.

### Gap 5 â€” Vendor, retention, deletion, and security controls

- **Obligation:** GDPR Articles 25, 28, and 32 and the storage-limitation principle require privacy by design, suitable processor terms, appropriate security, and deletion when data is no longer needed. International transfers require a Chapter V mechanism.
- **Current state:** Copies may persist in Tally, n8n execution data, Google Sheets/Docs, model-provider logs, and the Railway-hosted application. There is no single deletion or access-control procedure.
- **Required state:** MIRA must know where data resides, who can access it, how long each copy remains, and how deletion or access requests propagate across vendors.
- **Remediation:** Sign and catalogue processor agreements; document sub-processors and data regions; review transfer mechanisms; restrict administrator access; separate test and production data; encrypt in transit and at rest; protect API secrets; suppress unnecessary n8n payload logging; propose automatic deletion of raw uploads after 30 days unless the user chooses a shorter period or a justified project period requires longer; and test a complete deletion request across every system.
- **Escalation needed?** **Yes â€” DPO/privacy counsel and security lead.**

### Gap 6 â€” Operator guidance and substantiated claims

- **Obligation:** Article 4 requires providers and deployers to take measures, to their best extent, to ensure sufficient AI literacy for staff and others operating the system. Consumer-protection rules also prohibit misleading claims and material omissions.
- **Current state:** The MVP provides human review but no written review checklist, limitation statement, override procedure, or rule against using the creative profile for consequential assessments.
- **Required state:** Professional users must understand what the system does, its foreseeable errors and stereotypes, when disclosure is required, and that outputs are optional creative suggestions.
- **Remediation:** Add a short operator guide and onboarding: verify factual details, check for stereotypes and unwanted sensitive inferences, obtain permission for uploaded images, reject unsuitable output, never use MIRA for recruitment or eligibility decisions, retain required labels, and record final human approval. Remove or substantiate claims suggesting guaranteed accuracy, uniqueness, or commercial success.
- **Escalation needed?** **No** for the first draft; legal review should approve public claims and terms before launch.

## Reinforce â€” retrospective design review

The earlier design decision that creates the greatest compliance burden is routing identifiable photographs and detailed identity responses through several connected services while retaining intermediate copies. That choice increases the number of processors, possible international transfers, access points, retention settings, and deletion steps. If rebuilding the MVP, I would begin with text-only creative direction, make image upload optional, use a direct secured upload path instead of copying images into spreadsheets or general-purpose documents, isolate temporary processing from the long-term report, and automatically delete raw uploads after the generation job. I would also select text and image vendors only after confirming their data-use terms, EU processing options, retention controls, and Article 50 provenance capabilities.

## Phase 6 â€” Compliance memo

**To:** Head of Product, MIRA  
**From:** Project compliance reviewer  
**Date:** 18 August 2026  
**Subject:** First-pass EU AI Act assessment of the MIRA MVP

MIRA should be treated as a **limited-risk AI system subject to transparency duties**, rather than a prohibited or high-risk system. Its intended purpose is personal-brand and photoshoot planning, which is outside the eight Annex III high-risk areas. It generates recommendations and synthetic images but does not make decisions about employment, education, credit, public services, law enforcement, migration, justice, or democratic participation. Articles 50(1)â€“(2) are therefore the main AI Act provisions, with Article 50(4) also relevant when a generated image resembles a real person and could appear authentic.

If MIRA is offered under its own name, the MIRA entity is likely the provider, and more specifically a downstream provider of an integrated AI system. A photographer, studio, or agency using MIRA professionally is a deployer. The text- and image-model vendors remain providers of their respective models or AI components. Form, workflow, storage, document, and hosting vendors are not automatically AI Act providers, but they are likely to have processor or sub-processor roles under GDPR. No importer has been identified. Final role allocation must be confirmed from the commercial structure, contracts, branding, and exact model architecture.

The first key gap is user disclosure. AI involvement may be suggested by the product description, but MIRA has no documented requirement for a clear notice at first interaction. Second, the project has not verified whether generated text and images carry machine-readable AI provenance or whether the workflow preserves it. Third, photorealistic Brand World images are not consistently labelled for people who might mistake them for real photographs. Alongside the AI Act, MIRA has material GDPR work: the MVP lacks a complete data inventory, lawful-basis analysis, privacy notice, processor and transfer review, retention schedule, deletion workflow, and DPIA screening.

Before any public launch, the product team should act in this order. First, freeze the intended purpose and prohibit employment, eligibility, biometric-emotion, or other consequential repurposing without a fresh legal assessment. Second, implement the first-interaction notice and visible labels for generated concept images. Third, complete the model/vendor inventory and test machine-readable provenance through the full export path. Fourth, build the GDPR data map, privacy notice, processor register, retention/deletion process, and security controls. Fifth, provide a short operator guide covering human review, limitations, disclosure, and prohibited uses. AI regulatory counsel should confirm Articles 50(2) and 50(4); a DPO or privacy lawyer should review the GDPR package and DPIA threshold.

This memo is a first-pass educational assessment based on the MVP description available on the assessment date. It is not a legal opinion, conformity assessment, certification, or guarantee of compliance. It does not assess intellectual-property rights in uploaded references or generated images, detailed contract terms, national consumer law, or the actual technical behaviour of an unnamed model version.

